              "type": "text",
              "text": "Solve the following. You may assume the identity ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "\\cos3\\theta=4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p57-q12-14-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$x^3-3x-1=0$ using $x=2\\cos\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right),\\ 2\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{9}\\right),\\ 2\\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{9}\\right)$.",
          "worked": "Let $x=2\\cos\\theta$.\n$$\\begin{align*}8\\cos^3\\theta-6\\cos\\theta-1&=0\\\\2(4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta)-1&=0\\\\2\\cos3\\theta-1&=0\\\\\\cos3\\theta&=\\frac12\\\\3\\theta&=2k\\pi\\pm\\frac{\\pi}{3},\\quad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\\end{align*}$$\nChoosing $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{9},\\frac{5\\pi}{9},\\frac{11\\pi}{9}$ gives\n$$x=2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{9}\\right),\\quad2\\cos\\left(\\frac{5\\pi}{9}\\right),\\quad2\\cos\\left(\\frac{11\\pi}{9}\\right).$$\nThese are three distinct roots, so they are all the roots of the cubic.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q12-14-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$2x^3-3x-1=0$ using $x=\\sqrt2\\cos\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\sqrt2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right),\\ \\sqrt2\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right),\\ \\sqrt2\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\right)$.",
          "worked": "Let $x=\\sqrt2\\cos\\theta$.\n$$\\begin{align*}4\\sqrt2\\cos^3\\theta-3\\sqrt2\\cos\\theta-1&=0\\\\\\sqrt2(4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta)-1&=0\\\\\\sqrt2\\cos3\\theta-1&=0\\\\\\cos3\\theta&=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{2}\\\\3\\theta&=2k\\pi\\pm\\frac{\\pi}{4},\\quad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\\end{align*}$$\nChoosing $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{12},\\frac{3\\pi}{4},\\frac{7\\pi}{12}$ gives\n$$x=\\sqrt2\\cos\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\right),\\quad\\sqrt2\\cos\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\right),\\quad\\sqrt2\\cos\\left(\\frac{7\\pi}{12}\\right).$$\nThese are three distinct roots, so they are all the roots of the cubic.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 80
      },
      {
        "id": "p57-q12-14-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$x^3-6x^2+8=0$ using $x=\\sec\\theta$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=\\sec\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right),\\ \\sec\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}\\right),\\ \\sec\\left(\\frac{8\\pi}{9}\\right)$.\n\nMultiply the substituted equation by $\\cos^3\\theta$ to obtain $\\cos3\\theta=-\\frac12$.",
          "worked": "Let $x=\\sec\\theta$, where $\\cos\\theta\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\sec^3\\theta-6\\sec^2\\theta+8&=0\\\\1-6\\cos\\theta+8\\cos^3\\theta&=0\\\\2(4\\cos^3\\theta-3\\cos\\theta)+1&=0\\\\2\\cos3\\theta+1&=0\\\\\\cos3\\theta&=-\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nFor $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$,\n$$\\begin{align*}3\\theta&=\\frac{2\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{4\\pi}{3},\\ \\frac{8\\pi}{3}\\\\\\theta&=\\frac{2\\pi}{9},\\ \\frac{4\\pi}{9},\\ \\frac{8\\pi}{9}.\\end{align*}$$\nHence\n$$x=\\sec\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{9}\\right),\\quad\\sec\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{9}\\right),\\quad\\sec\\left(\\frac{8\\pi}{9}\\right).$$\nAll three secants are defined and distinct, so these are all the roots of the cubic.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 112
      }
    ]
  },
